ADVSYS -- a sample adventure for advcom and advint==================================================RECOVERED 2026-08-22.  `advint' plays a compiled ADVSYS world and `advcom'compiles one, and this disk shipped neither a world nor anything to compile:there was no .adi and no .adv anywhere on it, so both programs had nothing todo.  These two files were still in the original archive, play/h4/ARR/advint.ar.    osample.adv    a very small sample adventure, by David Betz    objects.adi    the object definitions it includesTWO LIMITS IN THE PROGRAMS shape how you have to call it:  * advcom holds a filename in 20 characters (FMAX in advint.h) and appends    `.adv' to it, so the name you give it must be 15 characters or fewer.    /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample is 24 and fails with "can't open input file".  * the `@objects.adi' include is opened relative to the DATA DIRECTORY, not    to the source file, so objects.adi has to be where you already are.With a shell whose `chd' is real that is three lines:    chd /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS    advcom osample                  writes osample.dat    advint osampleBASH CAN DO IT TOO -- corrected 2026-08-29.  This file used to say bash couldnot, because its `cd' tracks the path as a string and never moves the OS-9data directory (see DOC/README-RUNNING).  That is true and it does not matter:neither limit above is about changing directory, only about where the filesare and how long the name is.  Bring both files to the data directory you arealready in and use the short name:    cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/osample.adv > /dd/osample.adv    cat /dd/GAMES/ADVSYS/objects.adi > /dd/objects.adi    advcom osample                  writes osample.dat, 6015 bytes    advint osampleMeasured that day: it compiles, and the game plays -- you start in thelivingroom, `n' takes you to the hallway.advint.ar also carried an osample.dat, already compiled.  It is NOT here: thisadvint rejects it as "not an adventure data file", and the one advcom buildsfrom the same source is a different size.  It was made by another version, sothe source is the thing worth keeping.